        "anchor": "#replace-the-hierarchy-with-composition"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-045",
      "startMilliseconds": 534829,
      "endMilliseconds": 546760,
      "kind": "reason",
      "text": "Inheritance should be used as little as possible, so this animal-capability scenario is better modelled with composition.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 534829,
          "endMilliseconds": 546760
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#replace-the-hierarchy-with-composition"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-046",
      "startMilliseconds": 539440,
      "endMilliseconds": 546760,
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "The preferred alternative to inheritance here is composition: build an animal out of modules that each provide one capability.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 539440,
          "endMilliseconds": 546760
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#replace-the-hierarchy-with-composition"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-047",
      "startMilliseconds": 546760,
      "endMilliseconds": 563829,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "A creature module provides swimming and another provides flying, and these modules are handed to the animal together with the shared Animal data and its name, so name handling can be shared without inheritance.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 546760,
          "endMilliseconds": 563829
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#replace-the-hierarchy-with-composition"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-048",
      "startMilliseconds": 563829,
      "endMilliseconds": 575000,
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "With composition the concrete Penguin and Sparrow types disappear, so creation moves into factory functions that assemble an Animal plus whichever modules fit.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 563829,
          "endMilliseconds": 575000
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#assemble-animals-with-factories"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-049",
      "startMilliseconds": 575000,
      "endMilliseconds": 590000,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "A penguin factory sets the Animal data and gives it only the swim module, and the module type itself can be a base type or depend on data passed into the factory.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 575000,
          "endMilliseconds": 590000
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#assemble-animals-with-factories"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-050",
      "startMilliseconds": 590000,
      "endMilliseconds": 623949,
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "A second factory creates a sparrow with the correct values and with the fly module instead of the swim module, showing the same construction pattern reused per capability.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 590000,
          "endMilliseconds": 623949
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#assemble-animals-with-factories"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-051",
      "startMilliseconds": 618720,
      "endMilliseconds": 639670,
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "A bird such as a sparrow cannot swim but can fly while a duck does both, so abilities do not follow from the bird type and cannot be extracted by checking that type.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 618720,
          "endMilliseconds": 639670
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#read-capabilities-from-fields"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-052",
      "startMilliseconds": 639670,
      "endMilliseconds": 659839,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "Composition solves this by making the abilities fields of a more general type such as Animal, so the general code has to read fields instead of extracting anything from the bird type.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 639670,
          "endMilliseconds": 659839
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#read-capabilities-from-fields"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-053",
      "startMilliseconds": 659839,
      "endMilliseconds": 679590,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "The ability objects may be stored in a single array, but no inheritance is used in this design, so the shared methods simply work as they are on whatever ability type is in the field.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 659839,
          "endMilliseconds": 679590
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#read-capabilities-from-fields"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-054",
      "startMilliseconds": 679590,
      "endMilliseconds": 699600,
      "kind": "code_change",
      "text": "Because the field may hold nothing, the code first checks whether the ability is present and only then calls the method, either by checking a variable or by testing the field directly.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 679590,
          "endMilliseconds": 699600
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-055",
      "startMilliseconds": 699600,
      "endMilliseconds": 706120,
      "kind": "code_change",
      "text": "The same check is written more briefly with an if and an is pattern that repeats the general type name and binds the field value to a pattern variable used for the call.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 699600,
          "endMilliseconds": 706120
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-056",
      "startMilliseconds": 706120,
      "endMilliseconds": 714320,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "Although the is pattern looks like a type check, it only checks that the field is not null, and when the type is already known from the left-hand side no type check happens at all.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 706120,
          "endMilliseconds": 714320
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-057",
      "startMilliseconds": 714320,
      "endMilliseconds": 731790,
      "kind": "code_change",
      "text": "You can write empty braces with an is not null pattern instead, which performs the not-null check and on success puts the address stored in the field into the pattern variable.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 714320,
          "endMilliseconds": 731790
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-058",
      "startMilliseconds": 731790,
      "endMilliseconds": 752830,
      "kind": "reason",
      "text": "The advantage of the is not null pattern is that the behaviour no longer depends on any concrete ability type, so the same generic code keeps working as abilities change.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 731790,
          "endMilliseconds": 752830
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-059",
      "startMilliseconds": 752830,
      "endMilliseconds": 769510,
      "kind": "reason",
      "text": "Abilities such as flying and swimming belong to the separate ability modules rather than to the bird type, which makes the shared code reusable in many places, something inheritance cannot reproduce without repeating the shared code.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 752830,
          "endMilliseconds": 769510
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#check-for-a-module-before-calling-it"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-060",
      "startMilliseconds": 769510,
      "endMilliseconds": 777839,
      "kind": "code_change",
      "text": "The inheritance alternative is sketched by giving Penguin the ability to swim, adding Sparrow which can fly, and starting to add Duck.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 769510,
          "endMilliseconds": 777839
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-061",
      "startMilliseconds": 770040,
      "endMilliseconds": 781670,
      "kind": "fact",
      "text": "In the inheritance sketch Penguin is a non-flying Animal that swims, Sparrow is an Animal that flies, and Duck is an Animal that both flies and swims.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 770040,
          "endMilliseconds": 781670
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-062",
      "startMilliseconds": 781670,
      "endMilliseconds": 800230,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "Each bird overrides the behaviour it needs, so the overridden function may be empty in the demo but could contain printing or data computation in real code.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 781670,
          "endMilliseconds": 800230
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-063",
      "startMilliseconds": 800230,
      "endMilliseconds": 814710,
      "kind": "scope_boundary",
      "text": "Moving the shared logic into a static function instead of overriding it is possible, but it abandons inheritance and adds methods only to duplicate the common code.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 800230,
          "endMilliseconds": 814710
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-064",
      "startMilliseconds": 814710,
      "endMilliseconds": 854720,
      "kind": "reason",
      "text": "Calling the shared code through a general Animal variable is impossible when the function is defined separately in Penguin and in Duck, so a common base type is needed whose behaviour the birds inherit.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 814710,
          "endMilliseconds": 854720
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-065",
      "startMilliseconds": 845839,
      "endMilliseconds": 869150,
      "kind": "explanation",
      "text": "With inheritance Penguin takes the swimming function and the other functions from a base class, which raises the question of whether the birds could even inherit from two types at once.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 845839,
          "endMilliseconds": 869150
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-066",
      "startMilliseconds": 869150,
      "endMilliseconds": 884360,
      "kind": "reason",
      "text": "Trying to model the birds with inheritance produces a pile of similar classes and forces you to duplicate shared code, because a class cannot inherit from two classes.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 869150,
          "endMilliseconds": 884360
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "TP-067",
      "startMilliseconds": 884360,
      "endMilliseconds": 899519,
      "kind": "edge_case",
      "text": "Interfaces can partly solve this, but you still cannot simply inherit the function through them and have to redefine it anyway.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "type": "transcript_range",
          "startMilliseconds": 884360,
          "endMilliseconds": 899519
        }
      ],
      "englishCoverage": {
        "status": "covered",
        "anchor": "#why-the-inheritance-alternative-becomes-awkward"
      }
    }
